\section*{Exponential Algorithms for Knapsack}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 138; 2026-10-04), the asserted result is that bounded-error classical Subset Sum on polynomial-bit inputs has a uniform $O(2^{0.49n})$ algorithm with that bound on every random execution.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Manuscript 1.}
For positive integers $a_1,\ldots,a_n$, nonnegative target $t$ and $n\ge2$, ask whether $\sum_{i\in I}a_i=t$ for some subset. Put $b=\max\{1,\lceil\log_2(1+\max(t,a_1,\ldots,a_n))\rceil\}$ and use the stated word-RAM with read-only input and $w=\lceil4(n+b+\log_2(n+2))\rceil$-bit words; all preprocessing, addressing, word arithmetic and random generation are charged. The claimed single uniform randomized algorithm halts on every execution, is correct with probability at least $2/3$ on every input, and obeys
\[
 \forall c\ge1\ \exists C_c,N_c\ \forall n\ge N_c,\ b\le n^c:
 \quad T\le C_c2^{0.49n}
\]
word operations on every random path. The program is independent of the fixed polynomial bit-length exponent $c$.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 138; 2026-10-04), filtered representations recover modular aliases through small digit lists. Input-independent pair scores satisfy weighted Fourier-moment bounds, and checksum estimation combines direct sums with importance sampling; deterministic caps control both the visited lists and numerical work.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The new randomized algorithm has exponent $0.49$ for polynomial-bit Subset Sum inputs. The problem requires a deterministic exponent-one-third target with the broader encoded-input scope; the advertised improvement reaches neither that exponent nor that model.

\subsection{Formal verification}
No Lean formalization is listed at this snapshot for manuscript 1 (family 138; 2026-10-04).

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

\subsection{Sources}
